# Code owners for the Inkorporated monorepo.
#
# What this file enforces. With "Require review from Code Owners" enabled on the
# trunk's branch protection, GitHub will not accept a pull request until the owner
# named here has reviewed it, so a change to a safety-critical tree cannot land on
# the author's own approval. Two rules the docs already state, and that nothing
# used to check, become enforceable through this file:
#
#   - AI-assisted drafts are human-reviewed before merge
#     (docs/CONTRIBUTING.md, "Page structure");
#   - CRITICAL / Family Office cyborg personas keep human approval and restricted
#     invokers (AGENTS.md section 9; the per-persona keys are
#     `human_approval_required` and `allowed_invokers` in cyborgs/<JOB_ID>.yaml).
#
# The catch-all is what makes an owner review unavoidable repo-wide, including for
# a docs-only pull request written by a cyborg. The rules below it name the trees
# where that review has to be deliberate rather than incidental: provisioning,
# configuration, GitOps, the persona specs, and the CI that gates all of it.
#
# Patterns are gitignore-style and matched top to bottom; the last match wins.
# Flow and review expectations: CONTRIBUTING.md. Trunk, branch prefixes, and
# promotion by tag: docs/guides/git_workflow.md. Safety invariants that no review
# can waive: docs/project-conventions.md section 14.

# Everything without a more specific rule still needs the repository owner.
* @toxicoder

# Machine agent personas. A persona prompt is an executable policy: it names the
# tools the agent may use and the side effects it may take.
# TODO(owner): wants a security/Family Office steward who reads
# docs/cyborgs/schema.md and approves CRITICAL side effects and invoker lists.
cyborgs/ @toxicoder

# Family Office (PERS*) personas hold the FO data boundary; treat these as
# CRITICAL regardless of the security level the YAML declares.
cyborgs/PERS*.yaml @toxicoder

# Global configuration, environment values, and the domain template the Ingress
# manifests render from. This is where the no-hardcoded-domains rule lives.
config/ @toxicoder

# The flag registry: every flag off by default, with one owner, one expiry, and
# one reader. A flag that defaults on is a review failure, not a preference.
config/flags.yaml @toxicoder

# Provisioning. Plan-affecting edits are reviewed line by line.
# TODO(owner): wants a platform/Proxmox-and-cloud infra owner.
infrastructure/terraform/ @toxicoder

# Host and k3s configuration. Lint lane:
# bazelisk test //infrastructure/ansible:ansible_lint.
# TODO(owner): wants a platform/Ansible owner.
infrastructure/ansible/ @toxicoder

# GitOps source of truth for the clusters; Ingress here is checked by
# bazelisk test //:validate_domain_config.
# TODO(owner): wants a GitOps/ArgoCD owner.
apps/ @toxicoder

# The gate itself: workflows, composite actions, dependabot, the PR and issue
# templates, and this file. A change here changes what "green" means, so it cannot
# be merged by the person who wrote it.
# TODO(owner): wants a CI/build owner.
.github/ @toxicoder
